    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$9$ sides.\n\nThe angle-sum equation also gives $n=16$, but its largest angle is $195^{\\circ}$, so that polygon is not convex.",
      "worked": "Let the polygon have $n$ sides. Its last angle is $120^{\\circ}+5(n-1)^{\\circ}$. Equate the sum of this arithmetic sequence to the interior-angle sum:\n$$\\begin{align*}\n180(n-2)&=\\frac{n}{2}\\bigl(240+5(n-1)\\bigr)\\\\\n360n-720&=5n^2+235n\\\\\n0&=5n^2-125n+720\\\\\n0&=n^2-25n+144\\\\\n0&=(n-9)(n-16).\n\\end{align*}$$\nThus $n=9$ or $n=16$. For $n=16$, the largest angle is $120^{\\circ}+5(15)^{\\circ}=195^{\\circ}$, contradicting convexity. For $n=9$, the largest angle is $160^{\\circ}$, and all angles are less than $180^{\\circ}$. Therefore the polygon has $9$ sides.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
